{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787032780,"nonce":"cwIA5xT6/ejk0UH85owFlx1KkXM=","message":"Done in a5786e4, EXCEPT the group-close clause — which was declined on purpose. See ADR 0039.\n\nShipped: 00051 drops alert_group_members and gm_current_idx, adds occ_group_live_idx (renamed case_group_live_idx by 00052), and membership is now alert_cases.group_id + ended_at IS NULL. Leave, Join and JoinMany are deleted; Recompute absorbed JoinMany's work; adapters.joinMembers became settleGroup. occ_one_open_idx makes the duplicate-alert defect structurally impossible.\n\nThe member events are RETIRED, not removed. Deleting them from the closed enum would make NewEventType reject thirteen months of persisted group.member_joined rows and make oto emit values its own generated client rejects. So the constants stay and stay decodable; AppendTimelineEvent — the single writer — refuses them.\n\nDECLINED: 'group close becomes no live case with this split key rather than last_activity_at'. Taken literally that deletes group_close_delay, so an alert that resolves and re-fires inside the window finds its generation gone and opens a NEW Slack thread that cannot be re-parented. The safety goal the clause states is already held: CanCloseAt refuses while counts.Live() \u003e 0 over a rollup re-derived inside CloseIdle's own transaction — and 00051 is what made that refusal TRUE rather than nominal, because the counts now derive from ended_at. ADR 0039 has the full argument, and internal/grouping/service/closeidle_test.go now pins Held==1 with a teeth-check proving it is not vacuous.\n\nIf you disagree, the ADR is the thing to overrule; the code change is small.","files":null}]}